<div class="article-body">

<h2 id="answer">The Short Answer</h2>

<p>Buy the <strong>Spider Farmer SF-1000</strong> if your growing area is 2x2 feet and you want the cheapest board that does the job. It is $70, it draws 100 real watts, and it has 5,800 reviews behind it. Buy the <strong>Mars Hydro TS-1000</strong> if you want a 3x3 footprint for vegetables or you plan to flower anything. It is $89.99 and draws 150 real watts.</p>

<p>The $20 gap is not the interesting part. The interesting part is what those numbers do to your electricity bill and your coverage, and that is what most comparison tables leave out.</p>

<h2 id="names">Both Are Called 1000. Neither Draws 1000 Watts.</h2>

<p>Here is the key insight. The "1000" in both product names is a marketing number from the era of blurple panels, when a light advertised itself by the watts it supposedly replaced. It has nothing to do with what you plug in.</p>

<p>What you actually plug in:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Spec</th><th>Spider Farmer SF-1000</th><th>Mars Hydro TS-1000</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Real draw</td><td>100W</td><td>150W</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Price</td><td>$70</td><td>$89.99</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stated coverage</td><td>2x2 ft</td><td>2x2 flower / 3x3 veg</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Chips</td><td>Samsung LM301B</td><td>Samsung LM301B</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dimmable</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fan noise</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Amazon rating</td><td>4.6 from 5,800 reviews</td><td>4.6 from 3,600 reviews</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Same chip family. Same dimming. Same silence. One difference in that table actually changes what you can grow, and it is not the rating.</p>

<h2 id="power">What the Extra 50 Watts Costs You</h2>

<p>I run my lights 14 hours a day, which is the normal cycle for leafy greens. That makes the math easy:</p>

<p><strong>SF-1000 at 100W:</strong> 1.4 kWh a day, about <strong>42.6 kWh a month</strong>.<br/>
<strong>TS-1000 at 150W:</strong> 2.1 kWh a day, about <strong>63.9 kWh a month</strong>.</p>

<p>That is <strong>21.3 extra kWh every month</strong>, or roughly 255 kWh more over a year. Multiply that by your own rate and you have the real price of the upgrade. In most of the US that lands somewhere between two and four dollars a month, which sounds small until you remember the board itself only cost $20 more.</p>

<p>The honest truth is that the TS-1000 pays for its own electricity difference in coverage, not in savings. You are not buying a cheaper light. You are buying a bigger one.</p>

<h2 id="coverage">The Coverage Question Is the Real One</h2>

<p>A 100W board spread over 2x2 feet puts usable light on every plant. The same 100W spread over 3x3 feet is the same light stretched across more than twice the area, so the intensity at the leaves drops and the edges get weak.</p>

<p>That is exactly why the TS-1000 exists at 150W. Its 3x3 claim is the watts doing the work. If you already own the SF-1000 and you are wondering why the plants at the corners of a 3x3 tent are leggy and pale, the answer is not that Spider Farmer is bad. It is that you asked 100W to cover 9 square feet.</p>

<p>If you are still deciding whether to buy a light at all before you have a system, start with <a href="/posts/best-hydroponic-system-for-beginners">the hydroponic systems worth starting with</a>. If you want to build instead of buy, <a href="/posts/dwc-vs-kratky">DWC vs Kratky</a> shows both methods with real part costs.
</p>
<p>Coverage and wattage are one number, not two. Whatever light you buy, work it out first. Multiply the tent width by the height in feet to get square feet, then expect roughly 30 to 40 real watts per square foot for vegetables and herbs.</p>

<h2 id="pick">Which One Should You Buy</h2>

<p><strong>Buy the SF-1000 ($70)</strong> if your shelf or tent is 2x2. Lettuce, basil, cilantro, microgreens, seedlings. It is the cheapest correct answer, it is quiet, and it dims down for seedlings so you are not frying them in week one.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Spider+Farmer+SF-1000+LED+grow+light&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Check the SF-1000 price on Amazon</a></p>

<p><strong>Buy the TS-1000 ($89.99)</strong> if you want a 3x3 veg footprint, or you want to flower peppers, tomatoes, or anything fruiting. The 21.3 kWh a month is the cost of the extra space, and it buys you coverage the SF-1000 cannot reach.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Mars+Hydro+TS-1000+LED+grow+light&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Check the TS-1000 price on Amazon</a></p>

<p>Neither of these is the right light for a 4x4 tent. At that size you want 300W or more, and the <a href="/posts/best-led-grow-lights-for-beginners">full grow light comparison</a> covers what I would buy at each footprint. If you are still working out your photoperiod, <a href="/posts/how-much-light-does-lettuce-need">how much light lettuce actually needs</a> is the piece to read before you spend anything. And if you have not started growing yet, <a href="/posts/hydroponics-for-beginners">starting hydroponics without buying the wrong kit</a> comes first.</p>

